module

public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Variety.Projective.HyperplaneRestrictionPackageConstruction
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Variety.Projective.RegularitySectionMultiplicationFieldDescent
import StacksCore.Tactic.EgaSgaAttribute

/-!
# [Stacks, Tag 08A7] over an arbitrary field

`AlgebraicGeometry.span_mulSections_eq_top_of_isMRegular` proves [Stacks, Tag 08A7] — for an
`m`-regular coherent sheaf `ℱ` on `ℙⁿ_k` the multiplication map

`H⁰(ℙⁿ_k, ℱ(m)) ⊗_k H⁰(ℙⁿ_k, 𝒪(1)) ⟶ H⁰(ℙⁿ_k, ℱ(m+1))`

is surjective — from the hyperplane restriction data of
`AlgebraicGeometry.HyperplaneRestrictionPackage`, and
`AlgebraicGeometry.span_mulSections_eq_top_of_isMRegular_of_infinite` descends the conclusion from
an infinite field to an arbitrary one. This file composes the two with
`AlgebraicGeometry.nonempty_hyperplaneRestrictionPackage`, which produces that data over an
infinite field with no hypothesis.

## Main results

* `AlgebraicGeometry.span_mulSections_eq_top_of_isMRegular_field`: **[Stacks, Tag 08A7] over an
  arbitrary field**, with nothing assumed beyond `m`-regularity of the coherent sheaf.

## The mathematics

The proof is the composition of three results. The hypothesis `H` of
`AlgebraicGeometry.span_mulSections_eq_top_of_isMRegular_of_infinite` — the conclusion over every
infinite field — is `AlgebraicGeometry.span_mulSections_eq_top_of_isMRegular` applied to the
package of `AlgebraicGeometry.nonempty_hyperplaneRestrictionPackage`, and the descent to an
arbitrary field is the source's "we may assume `k` is infinite", carried out there by passing to
`k(t)`.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 08A7](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/08A7)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 08A4](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/08A4)
-/

@[expose] public noncomputable section

universe u

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- **[Stacks, Tag 08A7] over an arbitrary field.** Let `k` be a field, `n ≥ 0`, and let `ℱ` be a
coherent sheaf on `ℙⁿ_k` which is `m`-regular. Then the `k`-span of the products of global
sections of `ℱ(m)` with global sections of `𝒪(1)` is all of `H⁰(ℙⁿ_k, ℱ(m+1))`, i.e. the
multiplication map out of `H⁰(ℱ(m)) ⊗_k H⁰(𝒪(1))` is surjective.

`AlgebraicGeometry.span_mulSections_eq_top_of_isMRegular` proves this from the hyperplane
restriction data of [Stacks, Tag 08A7], which
`AlgebraicGeometry.nonempty_hyperplaneRestrictionPackage` supplies over an infinite field;
`AlgebraicGeometry.span_mulSections_eq_top_of_isMRegular_of_infinite` is the source's reduction to
that case.

Surjectivity of a `k`-bilinear multiplication out of a tensor product is the same statement as the
span of the products being everything — the image of the induced linear map on
`H⁰(ℱ(m)) ⊗_k H⁰(𝒪(1))` is generated by the images of the pure tensors — so the conclusion below
is the source's, not a weakening of it. The field is arbitrary, as in the source: `[Infinite k]`
appears only inside the proof. -/
@[stacks 08A7, sga "6 XIII 1.3" "(ii), special case"]
theorem span_mulSections_eq_top_of_isMRegular_field (k : Type u) [Field k] (n : ℕ)
    (F : (projectiveSpaceOverRing n (.of k)).Modules) [F.IsCoherent] (m : ℤ)
    (hF : IsMRegular k n F m) :
    mulSectionsSpan k n F m = ⊤ :=
  span_mulSections_eq_top_of_isMRegular_of_infinite
    (fun K _ _ N G hGc d hG =>
      span_mulSections_eq_top_of_isMRegular K
        (fun N' G' hG'c => nonempty_hyperplaneRestrictionPackage K N' G' hG'c) N G hGc d hG)
    k n F m hF

end AlgebraicGeometry

end
